  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 216 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nFocus turns to the potential for snow Wednesday night into \nThanksgiving. The forecast is becoming more clear as we enter the \nrealm of high-resolution guidance, but it is still malleable and \nsubject to shift. Here's what's consistent so far. Global model \nconsensus is that a northern stream upper level closed low will \ndeepen in the Northern Plains as it dives southeastward toward the \nMid-Mississippi Valley. It will then take on a negative tilt as it's \neither influenced by or phases with a southern stream closed low or \nshortwave trough. This will increase upper level support as rich \nmoisture advects into the region from the south and as mid-level \nfrontogenesis strengthens over Illinois. A narrow frontogenetical \nband of snow is forecast to develop here before sunrise on \nThanksgiving, though exactly where is still uncertain. CSI is \nevident within the band when analyzing EPV and sounding data. This, \npaired with ensemble probabilities of 0.25\"/6 hrs QPF near 50% \nincrease confidence in snowfall rates of 1\"+ within the heart of the \nband. Snowfall rates this high are more than enough to negate warm \nground temperatures and allow snow to accumulate. They also result \nin dynamic cooling, which will drop temperatures below the freezing \npoint and increase SLRs. Latest RRFS soundings at KSLO depict a deep \n(3000 ft) isothermal layer right at the freezing mark, which in \ntheory would promote riming and decrease SLRs. However, if dynamic \ncooling is able to cool the profile by about 2 degrees within the \nheart of the band, it will actually promote the aggregation of \nsnowflakes and further increase SLRs locally. The NBM 90th \npercentile accumulated snowfall shows amounts of 3-5\" in south-\ncentral Illinois. Though this footprint is too broad, it does give a \ngood idea of what can be expected within the band.\n\nOutside of the band, mesoscale dynamics will be much different. The \naforementioned warm ground and marginal air temperatures are \nexpected to play a much larger role in reducing SLRs and by \nextension snow accumulations. There is also plenty of\nvariability regarding the track of the upper level low, which \nwill impact how far west moisture is able to advect and how long\nthe chance of precipitation lasts in the area as a whole. \nModels that show the closed low phasing with the southern stream\ntrough also show a better potential for snow farther west as \nwell as a higher potential for wrap-around rain occurring \nThanksgiving afternoon. Though this would initially increase the\npotential for minor snow accumulations as far west as central \nMissouri in the morning, it also suggests that afternoon rain \nwould wash away much of the snow that sticks. Models that don't \nhave phasing in the upper levels also tend to show a more \nneutrally tilted closed low and keep moisture advection and much\nof the snow farther east into Illinois. In this scenario, snow \nwould be less likely to accumulate outside of the \nfrontogenetical band, and the chance of rain Thanksgiving \nafternoon decreases. Overall, 1-3\" of snow is possible in areas \nclosest to the band. If these totals do occur, melting and \ncompaction will ensure they do not last long.\n\nTo conclude, there is still plenty of variability regarding what \nthis system may or may not produce. Confidence is very high in a \nfrontogenetical band developing somewhere in Illinois, but exactly \nwhere is uncertain. Where this occurs is where impacts to travel \nwill be highest, though most won't be driving when the snow is \nfalling before sunrise. Snow totals outside of the band are highly \ndependent on environmental factors, many of which favor wet, \nmelting, compacting, and ultimately short-lived snow. By Friday \nmorning, precipitation will be exiting if not already gone, thus \nending the potential for snow through at least the next several days \nafter.",
